Accepted new evidence.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Fed - Overall Financial Well-Being","publisher":"Federal Reserve Board","publication_date":"2023-05-22","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/2023-economic-well-being-of-us-households-in-2022-overall-financial-well-being.htm","claim":"At the end of 2022, 73 percent of adults said they were doing at least okay financially, down 5 percentage points from 2021, and financial well-being gaps by race and ethnicity remained large.","locator":"lines 307-308 and 326-334","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Federal Reserve household survey summary with direct subgroup comparisons.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about household financial conditions in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual rather than directly presidential because inflation, asset prices, and labor-market conditions are major confounders.","contrary_evidence":["Strong labor markets coexisted with lower self-reported well-being.","The report does not isolate Biden-specific causation."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National Survey: Families Faced Financial Strain When Expanded CTC Expired","publisher":"Urban Institute","publication_date":"2023-08-15","url":"https://www.urban.org/research/publication/national-survey-families-faced-financial-strain-when-expanded-ctc-expired","claim":"Families reported a harder time affording essentials after the expanded Child Tax Credit expired, with more financial stress and food hardship.","locator":"lines 223-223","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable policy research organization using survey evidence; secondary but specific to the expiration effect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the post-expiration 2022 household period.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly related to Biden-era policy because the expanded credit was a federal policy that had expired by then.","contrary_evidence":["Survey timing and selection matter.","This describes the expiration effect, not a 2022 Biden action."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bridge Formula Program (BFP) Implementation Guidance","publisher":"Federal Highway Administration","publication_date":"2022-01-14","url":"https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/bridge/20220114.cfm","claim":"FHWA implemented the Bridge Formula Program with $5.5 billion per fiscal year for FY2022-FY2026, distributed to states by statutory formula for bridge replacement, rehabilitation, preservation, protection, and construction.","locator":"purpose and background sections","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary implementation guidance with concrete funding and allocation rules.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 infrastructure implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's signature infrastructure law, though execution is shared with FHWA and the states.","contrary_evidence":["Funding allocation is not the same as completed infrastructure improvements.","Distributional effects are lagged and uneven."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Recent U.S. Inflation Experience","publisher":"St. Louis Fed","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.stlouisfed.org/annual-report/2023/essay","claim":"The essay found that 54 percent of workers experienced a decline in real wages between 2021 and 2022, and the median worker saw a 1.5 percent real wage decline.","locator":"paragraphs under 'Real Wage Growth Distribution Shifted to the Negative Range in 2022'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Federal Reserve research using BLS and CPS data with explicit distributional wage calculations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 wage and inflation effects.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual, because the result reflects inflation and labor-market dynamics beyond presidential control.","contrary_evidence":["Monetary policy is independent of the president.","The essay does not isolate the effect of Biden policies."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bolstered balance sheets: Assessing household finances since 2019","publisher":"Brookings Institution","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/bolstered-balance-sheets-assessing-household-finances-since-2019/","claim":"Brookings concluded that households' financial positions in 2022 were significantly improved relative to 2019, with stronger income support, asset prices, and debt paydown contributing to the result.","locator":"lines 227-231","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Policy research analysis synthesizing household balance-sheet and income trends; strong for context, less direct on causation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the 2022 household-finance position relative to the pre-pandemic baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to federal pandemic-era transfers and labor-market recovery, but not uniquely to Biden in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The article stresses multiple drivers, including asset prices and spending constraints.","It is not a causal estimate of Biden-specific policy effects."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"US inflation eroded 2022 wages, as child poverty jumped, Census says","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2023-09-12","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/us-official-poverty-rate-was-115-in-2022-census-bureau-says-3172705","claim":"Reuters reported that inflation-adjusted income fell in 2022, supplemental child poverty rose to 12.4 percent, the official poverty rate remained 11.5 percent, and real median household income fell by 2.3 percent.","locator":"lines 419-428","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on Census releases with direct numeric summary and context from officials.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 household income, poverty, and purchasing power.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed attribution: strongly affected by expired pandemic benefits and inflation, not just Biden policy.","contrary_evidence":["The same release notes some subgroup gains and a lower Black poverty rate.","Official poverty did not worsen even as the supplemental measure did."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child poverty in the US jumped and income declined in 2022 as coronavirus pandemic benefits ended","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2023-09-12","url":"https://apnews.com/article/623f928ddcceca2bc5e69948929d266c","claim":"AP reported that child poverty more than doubled in 2022, median household income declined, income inequality fell on a pre-tax basis but rose after taxes, and the end of pandemic-era child tax credits was a key driver.","locator":"lines 1752-1758 and 1780-1784","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent wire reporting that closely tracks the Census release and explains the policy context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 household outcomes and the post-pandemic policy unwind.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed; the article ties results to inflation and the expiration of federal pandemic benefits rather than a single Biden decision.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes record-low Black poverty and higher income for some low-education households.","Several effects stem from the expiration of earlier relief rather than fresh 2022 policy choices."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Trump and Harris both support a bigger child tax credit. But which families should get it?","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2024","url":"https://apnews.com/article/child-tax-credit-poverty-cc423366a1a6f2299fb23ed169045b7b","claim":"AP stated that Biden's 2021 child tax credit expansion cut child poverty in half by one measure, but the expansion lasted only a year and Congress declined to renew it, erasing those gains after expiration.","locator":"lines 1759-1760 and 1790-1792","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent reporting that directly connects the policy design, observed effect, and expiration.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 retrospective on a Biden policy still affecting 2022 households through expiration.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Biden's American Rescue Plan policy, but the loss of the benefit reflects congressional inaction as well.","contrary_evidence":["The article frames the policy as a temporary 2021 measure, not a 2022 enactment.","It does not prove the 2022 effect absent the broader pandemic context."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Inflation Reduction Act will reduce household energy insecurity-but it could do more","publisher":"Brookings Institution","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/the-inflation-reduction-act-will-reduce-household-energy-insecurity-but-it-could-do-more/","claim":"Brookings argued that the IRA would lower household energy insecurity through residential tax credits, rebates, and targeted incentives, while also noting that energy burdens were already much higher for low-income, Black, Native, renter, and child households.","locator":"lines 167-180","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Policy analysis with concrete distributional claims, but largely prospective rather than outcome-based.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 policy design and the distributional baseline it sought to address.","ruler_attribution":"Directly connected to Biden's signed law, though the outcomes were prospective in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The article says the IRA does not go far enough.","This is a projected effect, not a realized 2022 outcome."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PN1437 - Nomination of Shalanda D. Young for Executive Office of the President, 117th Congress (2021-2022)","publisher":"Congress.gov","publication_date":"2022-03-15","url":"https://www.congress.gov/nomination/117th-congress/1437","claim":"Shalanda D. Young was confirmed by the Senate on 2022-03-15 to serve as Director of the Office of Management and Budget by a 61-36 vote.","locator":"latest action and description lines 489-497","source_type":"official/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary congressional nomination record with final confirmation action and vote tally.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 ruler period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's personnel choice and the Senate's confirmation; OMB is a central implementation hub for the economic agenda.","contrary_evidence":["Confirmation alone does not prove policy success.","A single appointment is only partial evidence for the broader economics team."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: President Biden Signs Executive Order to Implement the CHIPS and Science Act of 2022","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-25","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-president-biden-signs-executive-order-implement-the-chips-and-science-act-2022","claim":"The executive order created a CHIPS Implementation Steering Council and set implementation priorities including taxpayer protection, domestic capacity, regional clusters, private investment, and benefits for disadvantaged and rural communities.","locator":"lines 63-97","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Secondary transcript of an official White House fact sheet; strong on the implementation structure, weaker than a primary archive page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 implementation of a major Biden law.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's executive action and implementation choices.","contrary_evidence":["This is an implementation plan, not an outcome study.","It reflects administration intent rather than measured results."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.4","5B.6","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FACT SHEET: CHIPS and Science Act Will Lower Costs, Create Jobs, Strengthen Supply Chains, and Counter China","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-08-09","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fact-sheet-chips-and-science-act-will-lower-costs-create-jobs-strengthen-supply-chains-and","claim":"The White House said Biden's industrial strategy had added 642,000 manufacturing jobs since 2021 and that new manufacturing construction had increased 116 percent over the prior year as the CHIPS Act was signed.","locator":"lines 62-69","source_type":"other","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Secondary transcript of an official fact sheet with concrete numerical claims, but still administration framing.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 industrial-policy rollout.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden's industrial policy agenda, though the job-growth attribution is confounded by the broader post-pandemic recovery.","contrary_evidence":["The claim is administration framing rather than independent evaluation.","Manufacturing growth is partly attributable to cyclical recovery and private investment."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Raimondo seeks to rally US behind $52 billion chip program","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://apnews.com/article/c658a059c2b59813c6ea4f0617cbdaa1","claim":"AP reported that the CHIPS program would require training tens of thousands of workers and building semiconductor clusters, and that the initial $39 billion in factory funding was expected to leverage at least ten times that amount in private investment.","locator":"lines 1772-1807","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent reporting on an official implementation push with concrete policy and investment details.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 CHIPS implementation and expected effects.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Biden's signed CHIPS law, though the article reports expected rather than realized effects.","contrary_evidence":["The article emphasizes a multi-year challenge rather than completed results.","Private investment leverage is projected, not guaranteed."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}

Search ledger: candidates found about 60, documents opened 20, duplicates or near-duplicates collapsed 21, irrelevant or out-of-period items 8, inaccessible items 4, accepted distinct new URLs 12.

Material gaps and next best query/source directions:
- Direct 2022 implementation/outcome evidence for CHIPS and IRA is still stronger on intent and rollout than on realized effects. Next best sources: later CBO, Treasury, IRS, Commerce, and independent evaluations that quantify actual 2022-2023 uptake, investment, prices, and employment.
- Distributional evidence is still too aggregated for full chapter confidence. Next best sources: BEA distribution of personal income tables, Census post-tax/disposable income and SPM tables by race and region, and BLS wage percentile tables.
- Appointment evidence is better for OMB than for the broader economics team. Next best sources: confirmation records or official bios for Treasury, Commerce, NEC, CEA, IRS, and the CHIPS/IRA implementation leads.
- The record still needs more independent comparison of Biden-specific policy versus inherited recovery dynamics. Next best sources: IMF/OECD follow-ups, peer-reviewed studies on inflation, real wages, and industrial-policy spillovers, plus state-level distribution and purchasing-power adjustments.
